% ============================================================================
% insulin-glucagon-trigger — the blood-glucose condition that triggers each
% of the pancreas's two glucose-regulating hormones, as the SAME NCI SEER
% spans also state (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `hormone-glands.adj`
% (`hormone_gland(hormone, gland)`, WHICH gland secretes a hormone — insulin
% → pancreas, glucagon → pancreas, etc.). That table's own header already
% quotes, verbatim, the trigger condition for the pancreas's two glucose
% hormones, and buried in those two spans, alongside the gland the parent
% table already captures, is a second fact the hormone→gland schema had no
% room for: WHAT blood-glucose condition triggers each hormone's release.
%
%   insulin → high
%     "Beta cells in the pancreatic islets secrete the hormone insulin in
%      response to a high concentration of glucose in the blood."
%
%   glucagon → low
%     "Alpha cells in the pancreatic islets secrete the hormone glucagons in
%      response to a low concentration of glucose in the blood."
%     (the page spells it "glucagons"; the hormone is glucagon)
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? secretion_trigger(insulin, $Level)   % high
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, an ordinary two-column EXACT
% lookup): each `row` lowers to a ground relation `secretion_trigger(hormone,
% blood_glucose_level)` carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the
% ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the trigger condition WITH
% its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls. Only these two hormones
% among `hormone-glands.adj`'s twelve have a blood-glucose trigger stated in
% this cited span, so this sibling is HONESTLY NARROW: it grounds exactly
% the pancreas's two glucose-regulating hormones and abstains on every other
% hormone (thyroxine, cortisol, etc.), which the source never ties to a
% blood-glucose trigger at all. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a level
% and recall which hormone responds to it:
%
%     ? secretion_trigger($Hormone, low)
%       % glucagon
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME two NCI SEER Training
% Modules spans already quoted inside `hormone-glands.adj`'s own header — no
% new WebFetch, only a different schema decoding the trigger-condition half
% of each already-verified quote. `trust authoritative` — the same tier
% `hormone-glands.adj` already carries for this pair (U.S. National Cancer
% Institute, a primary U.S. government teaching resource).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table secretion_trigger {
    columns hormone, blood_glucose_level

    row (insulin, high)    % SEER: "Beta cells in the pancreatic islets secrete the hormone insulin in response to a high concentration of glucose in the blood."
    row (glucagon, low)    % SEER: "Alpha cells in the pancreatic islets secrete the hormone glucagons in response to a low concentration of glucose in the blood."

    source "Beta cells in the pancreatic islets secrete the hormone insulin in response to a high concentration of glucose in the blood."
    locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/endocrine/glands/pancreas.html"
    trust authoritative

    cites "Alpha cells in the pancreatic islets secrete the hormone glucagons in response to a low concentration of glucose in the blood." locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/endocrine/glands/pancreas.html"
}
